<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bury St Edmunds</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bury St Edmunds is a market town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It grew around the abbey that housed the relics of Edmund, the East Anglian king killed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> in 869, whose shrine became one of the great pilgrimage centres of medieval England. Tradition holds that in 1214 barons met in the abbey church to swear to force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> to accept the charter that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bury-st-edmunds.jpg" alt="Bury St Edmunds" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Fielding from Norwich, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bury_St_Edmunds_Abbey_(44231403370).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
